From: nix-wasm <eric@codebutler.com>
Subject: [PATCH] hush: a bare "exit" inside the EXIT (trap 0) handler, and "$?" read at the top of that handler, must reflect the PENDING exit status, not whatever the trap body's own commands left in $?

Applies on top of 0009-hush-variable-fd-redirect.patch +
0010-hush-internally-opened-fd0.patch (independent fix, same file,
unrelated code path — hush_exit()/builtin_exit(), not the redirect-
parsing functions those two touch; applies zero-fuzz with or without
them, see SCOPE below).

PROBLEM: found while root-causing a #193 in-guest MMU/fork soak finding.
The fixture's generated `configure`, run under the patched (0009+0010)
hush on the real booted guest, hit a genuinely fatal error mid-run
("configure: error: cannot compute suffix of executables: cannot compile
and link" — an unrelated in-guest cc kernel bug, #192), printed the error
correctly, and stopped — yet the harness's `sh ./configure; echo
CFGRC=$?` captured **CFGRC=0**. Under bash and dash, the identical
`./configure` run (same fixture, same sabotaged-compiler setup) exits
NONZERO (77, from `as_fn_error 77 "C compiler cannot create
executables"`). A configure script whose real fatal failure is reported
to callers as exit 0 is exactly the "universally-broken-exit-status"
class this repo already spent effort eliminating for #009/#010's own
gaps (#188/#189/#190) — it would false-pass any script/CI/Makefile that
only checks `$?` after running configure.

ROOT CAUSE: every autoconf-generated `configure` installs a trap that
runs on ordinary shell exit (signal 0), to flush config.log:

    ac_exit_trap ()
    {
      exit_status=
      ...
      eval "rm -f $ac_clean_CONFIG_STATUS core *.core core.conftest.*" &&
        rm -f -r conftest* confdefs* conf$$* $ac_clean_files &&
        exit $exit_status
    }
    trap 'ac_exit_trap $?' 0

`exit_status` is deliberately left UNSET/empty inside the function (this
is standard, correct autoconf code, not a bug in the generated script);
`exit $exit_status` therefore expands, after word-splitting drops the
empty word, to a completely bare `exit` with NO argument. POSIX and every
real shell (bash, dash) give a bare `exit` run from *inside* a trap
handler a special meaning: it does NOT mean "exit with the status of the
last command I, the trap body, happened to run" (which here would be 0,
from the successful `rm -f -r ...` right before it) — it means "finish
exiting with whatever exit status was already PENDING before this trap
started running" (i.e. the real, intended exit status: e.g. `as_fn_exit
77`'s 77). This is exactly the mechanism that makes autoconf's
`ac_exit_trap` idiom work at all: it is bookkeeping (flush config.log,
clean up temp files) bolted onto the *real* configure exit without
disturbing it.

hush already implements this "pending status" behavior CORRECTLY for
every ordinary signal trap, and it does so in TWO parts:
`check_and_run_traps()` (shell/hush.c) both (a) saves `G.last_exitcode`
into `G.pre_trap_exitcode` AND (b) leaves `G.last_exitcode` itself
holding that same pending value, before invoking a trap's
`builtin_eval()` — so `$?` read anywhere in the trap body, from the very
first statement, already reflects the pending status, and
`builtin_exit()`'s no-argument path explicitly prefers
`G.pre_trap_exitcode` when it is set (`>= 0`) for the "bare exit resumes
it" half:

    G.pre_trap_exitcode = save_rcode = G.last_exitcode;   /* both halves, before the trap body runs */
    builtin_eval(argv);
    ...
    if (G.pre_trap_exitcode >= 0) /* "exit" in trap uses $? from before the trap */
        hush_exit(G.pre_trap_exitcode);
    hush_exit(G.last_exitcode);

But `trap ... 0` (the EXIT pseudo-trap) is NOT run through
`check_and_run_traps()` at all — it is run by `hush_exit()` itself, at
the moment the shell is actually terminating, via a SEPARATE,
independently-written code path that calls `builtin_eval(argv)` directly
and (before this patch) touched NEITHER half of that state:

    if (G.exiting <= 0 && G_traps && G_traps[0] && G_traps[0][0]) {
        char *argv[3];
        argv[1] = xstrdup(G_traps[0]);
        argv[2] = NULL;
        G.exiting = 1; /* prevent EXIT trap recursion */
        builtin_eval(argv);
    }

`G.pre_trap_exitcode` is initialized once, at shell startup, to the
sentinel -1 ("not currently inside a trap") and (before this patch) was
otherwise touched only by `check_and_run_traps()`. So when a bare `exit`
ran inside the EXIT trap body, `builtin_exit()`'s `G.pre_trap_exitcode >=
0` test saw the untouched -1 sentinel, fell through to
`hush_exit(G.last_exitcode)`, and `G.last_exitcode` by then held whatever
the trap body's OWN commands last set it to (the successful `rm -f -r
...`'s 0) — not the real exit status (77) that was pending when the trap
fired. Two independent, inconsistent implementations of "what a trap
handler is" inside the same file — the EXIT (signal 0) trap and every
other signal trap — for what should be exactly the same "before the
trap" contract.

SECOND, INDEPENDENT SYMPTOM OF THE SAME ROOT CAUSE (review finding,
P2-1): because `G.last_exitcode` itself (not just `G.pre_trap_exitcode`)
went unset, it is not only the bare-`exit` idiom that was wrong — a `$?`
READ anywhere inside the EXIT trap body, even before any command of its
own runs, was ALSO wrong (still the pre-exit command's status, not the
real exit code). This breaks the equally common
`trap 'rc=$?; cleanup; exit $rc' 0` idiom (which autoconf itself
generates inside `config.status`'s own trap, coincidentally masked there
today only because `as_fn_exit` happens to pre-arrange a matching `$?` —
luck, not correctness):

    $ cat > t2.sh <<'EOF'
    trap 'exit_status=$?; exit $exit_status' 0
    exit 77
    EOF
    $ bash t2.sh; echo $?     # 77 (correct)
    $ dash t2.sh; echo $?     # 77 (correct)
    $ hush t2.sh; echo $?     # 0  (WRONG, with only the pre_trap_exitcode-only fix)

CONFIRMED PRE-EXISTING, NOT introduced by 0009/0010: both symptoms
reproduce byte-for-byte on PRISTINE busybox-1.36.1 (no patches at all —
the minimal repros involve no variable-fd redirect and no `<&0` at all,
so they exercise neither 0009's nor 0010's changed code). Minimal repro
of the bare-`exit` symptom (works under `hush -c`/`hush script`, no
configure/autoconf needed):

    $ cat > t.sh <<'EOF'
    ac_exit_trap ()
    {
      exit_status=
      rm -f -r /tmp/nonexistent 2>/dev/null &&
        exit $exit_status
    }
    trap 'ac_exit_trap $?' 0
    exit 5
    EOF
    $ bash t.sh; echo $?     # 5  (correct: bare exit-in-trap resumes 5)
    $ dash t.sh; echo $?     # 5  (correct)
    $ hush t.sh; echo $?     # 0  (WRONG, on both pristine and 0009+0010-patched hush)

UPSTREAM: confirmed still present, unfixed, in busybox git as of
2026-08-13 (checked against `github.com/mirror/busybox`, commit
`371fe9f71d445d18be28c82a2a6d82115c8af19d` — `git ls-remote`'d and its
`shell/hush.c` fetched directly in this session; `hush_exit()` there is
byte-for-byte the pristine 1.36.1 code shown above, neither half of this
fix present). Original fix, mirroring `check_and_run_traps()`'s own
precedent for every other trap.

FIX: set BOTH `G.pre_trap_exitcode` AND `G.last_exitcode` from the REAL,
precise exit status (`hush_exit()`'s own `exitcode` parameter — the value
the shell is actually about to terminate with, e.g. `as_fn_exit`'s `exit
$1`, or a plain top-of-script `exit N`, or `set -e`'s errexit path)
immediately before invoking the EXIT trap's `builtin_eval()`, exactly
mirroring `check_and_run_traps()`'s own `G.pre_trap_exitcode = save_rcode
= G.last_exitcode;` for ordinary signal traps — the difference being that
here `G.last_exitcode` is not ALREADY the right value going in (as it is
in `check_and_run_traps()`, which merely records what's already current),
so both fields must be written explicitly from `exitcode`. `exitcode` is
in fact a STRICTLY BETTER "before" value than reusing `G.last_exitcode`
would be here (the value `check_and_run_traps()` uses for signal traps,
where there is no associated exit-code argument to be more precise with)
— it is the exact argument `hush_exit()` was called with, not a proxy for
it. No restore is needed afterward (unlike `check_and_run_traps()`, which
returns to normal execution): `hush_exit()` never returns, so nothing
downstream ever reads the values this overwrites. Both masked with
`& 0xff` to match the 0-255 range every other exit-status field in this
file uses (`G.last_exitcode` is `smalluint`; `hush_exit()`'s own final
`sigexit(-(exitcode & 0xff))` / `_exit(exitcode)` call already implicitly
narrows the same way). `#if ENABLE_HUSH_TRAP` guards the whole block,
matching every other `G.pre_trap_exitcode` reference in the file (the
field does not exist when HUSH_TRAP is disabled) — busybox's standard
defconfig, and every wasm-guest defconfig in this repo, enables
`CONFIG_HUSH_TRAP=y`, so this is unconditionally live in every consumer.

VERIFIED (host-side, hermetic, native x86_64, no wasm involved): rebuilt
a native busybox with 0009+0010+this two-line fix and re-ran the FULL
matrix against bash/dash ground truth. Every row below is the shell's
raw exit status ($?) unless noted; "hush" = the 0009+0010+0011-patched
native build.

  Original repro + adjacent cases:
    trap 'ac_exit_trap $?' 0 (rm && bare exit); exit 5     bash=5   dash=5   hush=5
    (as_fn_exit-style; exit 0/1/77 via as_fn_set_status)   bash/dash/hush all match per-code (0, 1, 77)
    real fixture ./configure, working compiler              bash/dash/hush all 0
    real fixture ./configure, sabotaged always-failing cc    bash=77  dash=77  hush=77  (was hush=0 pre-fix)
    P1 dangling-redirect negative probe (`echo hi >&<x`)     hush=1 (unaffected — different function)
    trap body's LAST command fails (`false`) then bare exit  bash=5   hush=5   (resumes the real pending 5, not false's 1)
    EXIT trap calls `exit 33` explicitly                     bash=33  hush=33  (unaffected — never reads pre_trap_exitcode)
    no EXIT trap at all, `exit 42`                            bash=42  hush=42  (unaffected — block is gated on G_traps[0])

  P2-1 review addition — "$?" read INSIDE the trap body (the
  `rc=$?; ...; exit $rc` idiom), and the surrounding exit-status
  matrix the review asked to be re-run in full against this idiom's
  fix too, to prove it didn't regress anything the first cut got right:
    trap "echo dollar=\$?; exit" 0; true; exit 7    bash: dollar=7 rc=7   hush (pre-fix): dollar=0 rc=7   hush (post-fix): dollar=7 rc=7
    trap 'exit_status=$?; exit $exit_status' 0; exit 77   bash=77  dash=77  hush (pre-fix)=0  hush (post-fix)=77
    explicit `exit 9` (harmless EXIT trap present)          bash=9   hush=9
    EXIT trap body itself fails (`false`, no explicit exit); `exit 6`   bash=6   hush=6   (POSIX: trap not calling exit -> pending 6 wins over the trap's own failing last command)
    fall off end of script (no exit, last cmd `true`)        bash=0   hush=0
    fall off end of script, last cmd `false` (EOF)            bash=1   hush=1
    `set -e`; `false`                                          bash=1   hush=1
    `exit -2`                                                   bash=254 hush=254

  P3-2 review addition — subprocess-inheritance corner cases (a real
  fork() copies hush's global state, including the now-set
  G.pre_trap_exitcode, so a bare "exit" run in a CHILD spawned FROM
  inside the EXIT trap body also resumes the outer pending status —
  this was not designed, it falls out of fork()'s COW semantics, and
  is worth recording since it's now user-visible):
    trap 'v=$(exit); echo rc=$?' 0; exit 5           ($() child)      bash=5   dash=5   hush=5   (all agree)
    trap 'true | (exit); echo rc=$?' 0; exit 5        (pipeline child) bash=5   dash=5   hush=5   (all agree)
    trap '(false; exit); echo rc=$?' 0; exit 5        (subshell child) bash=1   dash=5   hush=5   (bash DISAGREES with dash here -- bash's subshell resumes its OWN last command (false=1), not the outer pending status; dash's subshell inherits the outer pending status and a bare exit there resumes IT. hush, having no independent concept of "am I a subshell descended from a trap" beyond the inherited memory state, now behaves like dash. This is a pre-existing bash/dash divergence in what a forked subshell's own pre_trap_exitcode state means, not something this patch invents; noted here because it is now empirically true of hush too, not verified before this patch (hush's own pre_trap_exitcode was always -1 before now, so no such inheritance could occur).
  NOMMU note: this inheritance is a property of a REAL fork() copying
  process memory (true on this native host build, and true of the
  fork/MMU guest variant's busybox-fork.nix). It does NOT apply to the
  NOMMU busybox.nix/ash.nix guest's own subshell/`$()`/pipeline
  mechanism (busybox's `!BB_MMU` "forkshell" re-exec path,
  `re_execute_shell()` etc.): a re-exec'd child there is a FRESH
  `main()` invocation of a new process image, not a copy of the
  parent's memory, so `G.pre_trap_exitcode` resets to its compiled-in
  -1 startup default in the child regardless of what the parent held
  (confirmed structurally: no `!BB_MMU` re-exec/serialization code path
  in shell/hush.c references `pre_trap_exitcode` at all). The headline
  `ac_exit_trap` path this patch exists for is a plain, non-forking
  function call either way, so this divergence does not affect it.

SCOPE: applies with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` cleanly on top of pristine
busybox-1.36.1 with 0009+0010 already applied (this repo's actual stacking
order in busybox.nix/busybox-fork.nix/ash.nix) AND standalone against
pristine busybox-1.36.1 with NEITHER 0009 nor 0010 applied (verified
both ways) — `hush_exit()`/`builtin_exit()` share no lines with the
redirect-parsing functions 0009/0010 touch. Wired into all three
derivations whose source tree carries shell/hush.c (busybox-fork.nix,
busybox.nix, ash.nix), each gaining a postPatch guard asserting the
shipped `hush_exit()` carries the `G.pre_trap_exitcode = exitcode &
0xff;` assignment BEFORE its `builtin_eval(argv);` call (not merely
present somewhere in the function body — a fuzzy apply that landed the
assignment AFTER `builtin_eval` would be a no-op that this stricter check
catches and a bare presence check would not), so a stacked patch apply
that silently drops or misplaces this hunk (the exact hazard
patches/kernel's 0017-0020 zero-fuzz lesson documents) fails the BUILD
loudly instead of shipping a hush whose EXIT-trap-guarded `configure`
runs silently regress to reporting success on failure. NOTE: ash.nix's
own `/bin/sh` is ash, not hush, and its `allnoconfig`-based .config never
sets `CONFIG_SHELL_HUSH=y` (only the `ASH_*`/`SHELL_ASH` family) — so
`shell/hush.c` is patched there (the source tree carries it, and the
postPatch guard above still runs) but never actually COMPILED into that
derivation's busybox binary. The patch and its guard are wired there for
source-tree consistency across all three busybox derivations, not
because ash.nix's own shell exercises this fix at runtime.

diff -ruN a/shell/hush.c b/shell/hush.c
--- a/shell/hush.c
+++ b/shell/hush.c
@@ -2091,6 +2091,31 @@
 		argv[1] = xstrdup(G_traps[0]); /* copy, since EXIT trap handler may modify G_traps[0] */
 		argv[2] = NULL;
 		G.exiting = 1; /* prevent EXIT trap recursion */
+#if ENABLE_HUSH_TRAP
+		/* Mirror what check_and_run_traps() does for every OTHER trap
+		 * (SIGINT etc): record the exit status that is "pending"
+		 * before the trap body runs, so that (a) a bare "exit" (no
+		 * argument) executed FROM WITHIN the trap body resumes THIS
+		 * status (POSIX: a trap that does not itself call exit with
+		 * an explicit status leaves the shell to exit with the
+		 * status that was already pending) instead of whatever
+		 * G.last_exitcode drifts to from ordinary commands the trap
+		 * body runs first (e.g. autoconf's ac_exit_trap: "rm -f -r
+		 * ... && exit $exit_status" with $exit_status empty -> bare
+		 * "exit" -> must resume the real exit code, not rm's 0), and
+		 * (b) "$?" read anywhere in the trap body -- even before the
+		 * trap runs any command of its own -- also reflects it (the
+		 * equally common "trap 'rc=$?; cleanup; exit $rc' 0" idiom).
+		 * Without this, builtin_exit()'s "G.pre_trap_exitcode >= 0"
+		 * check sees the -1 "not in a trap" sentinel (only
+		 * check_and_run_traps() ever sets it) and falls through to
+		 * hush_exit(G.last_exitcode) -- the wrong value; and a bare
+		 * "$?" read stays whatever the pre-exit command left it at.
+		 * hush_exit() never returns, so nothing downstream reads the
+		 * values this overwrites. */
+		G.pre_trap_exitcode = exitcode & 0xff;
+		G.last_exitcode = exitcode & 0xff;
+#endif
 		/* Note: G_traps[0] is not cleared!
 		 * "trap" will still show it, if executed
 		 * in the handler */
